          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Issachar most commonly signifies discernment of the times and wise action, while recognized alternatives include burden-bearing labor, hire or reward, settled prosperity, provision, and loyal service. The responses generally deny a universally settled meaning.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book combines the recognized motifs into one cumulative portrait: a hired yet established field-holder, strong and humble burden-bearer, settled tent-dweller, discerner of appointed times, royal provider, and courageous participant in Israel’s deliverance. It further identifies Issachar with Virgo, sapphire, and the full moon.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Issachar as an emblem of timely discernment and strategic wisdom, with labor, reward, rest, prosperity, and service as alternative traditions.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Issachar as a unified archetype joining hire, landed settlement, humble strength, burden-bearing, temporal discernment, provision, and courageous service, supplemented by celestial and gemstone correspondences.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus usually treats discernment as primary and the other biblical motifs as alternative or secondary readings. The book reclassifies those motifs as simultaneous aspects of one integrated identity and adds cases absent from the consensus: Virgo, sapphire, and the full moon.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction with the consensus’s principal or alternative biblical meanings. The disagreement concerns whether the motifs should be combined and whether the added celestial and gemstone identities belong to Issachar’s symbolism.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus core of understanding the times and incorporates nearly every listed alternative, so it does not replace the recognized referent. It materially broadens the identification by making separate traditions cumulative and adding celestial and mineral correspondences. That is a refinement by expansion rather than a match, divergence, or wholly novel reading.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed biblical passages substantially support the terrestrial traits—hire, donkey strength, bowing to burdens, tents, discernment, provision, and entering the valley—but they do not by themselves establish Virgo, sapphire, or the full moon. “Owns the field” and “rich enough to feed a king” also sharpen indirect textual associations into stronger symbolic claims.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
